<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Takamatsu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Takamatsu is a port city on the Seto Inland Sea, capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagawa-prefecture">Kagawa Prefecture</a> on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It grew around a sea castle begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikoma-chikamasa">Ikoma Chikamasa</a> in 1588, whose moats were fed by seawater, and was the seat of the Matsudaira lords of the Takamatsu Domain during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. It is known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ritsurin-garden">Ritsurin Garden</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/takamatsu.jpg" alt="Takamatsu" width="320" height="137" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dokudami · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanazyukai20220729_7a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Takamatsu is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagawa-prefecture">Kagawa Prefecture</a>, on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>. It lies on the Takamatsu Plain, part of the Sanuki Plain, and its northern part faces the Seto Inland Sea, forming a semicircular urban area centred on Takamatsu Port and Takamatsu New Port. The western part of the city is an alluvial fan formed by the Koto River, and the eastern part is a flood plain of the Kasuga and Shinkawa rivers.</p><p>In the north-east, Yashima is a table-shaped plateau protruding into the sea, the site of the Battle of Yashima in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a>, and Cape Takei is the northernmost tip of the main island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>. Parts of the city lie within the Setonaikai National Park, and it includes a number of small inhabited islands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area belonged to the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanuki-province">Sanuki Province</a>. During the Heian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> periods it was the closest port to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, a transportation centre and a gateway for pilgrims to the Kotohira Shrine. In the Sengoku period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikoma-chikamasa">Ikoma Chikamasa</a> built the first Takamatsu Castle in 1588.</p><p>In 1642 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-yorishige">Matsudaira Yorishige</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yorifusa">Tokugawa Yorifusa</a> of Mito Domain and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, was awarded the Takamatsu Domain, assessed at 120,000 koku, which his descendants ruled until the Meiji restoration.</p><p>The city was created on 15 February 1890, when the modern municipal system was established, and the castle tower that had been its symbol was destroyed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji period</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the XXI Bomber Command of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> chose Takamatsu as a target because it was a focal point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>'s rail and road transport and held some industry vital to the war effort.</p><p>On 3 July 1945, 128 B-29 bombers dropped over 800 tons of incendiary bombs, destroying 78 percent of the built-up area and killing 1,359 people. The city recovered quickly after the war and was designated a core city with greater local autonomy on 1 April 1999. In 2005 and 2006 it absorbed several neighbouring towns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Takamatsu is the largest municipality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>. It holds most of the national government's branch offices for the island and many companies' regional offices, and it is the home of the Shikoku Railway Company. The Seto-Ōhashi Bridge links its rail line to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>. Kagawa University and Takamatsu University are in the city. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 417,496 people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Takamatsu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In one of the battles of the Genpei War, fought in Sanuki Province on Shikoku, near present-day <b>Takamatsu</b>, the forces of the Taira clan were defeated by the Minamoto.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-22">22 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Ikoma Chikamasa received Sanuki from Toyotomi Hideyoshi in 1587 and in 1588 began <b>Takamatsu</b> castle, a sea castle with moats fed by seawater.</blockquote><a href="/state/takamatsu-domain">Takamatsu Domain</a></li></ul></section>
